@startuml flow-video-encryption
' How a recording is encrypted, and how a browser gets the key to play it.
'
' Render:  plantuml docs/diagrams/flow-video-encryption.puml

title Flow — encrypting a recording, and unlocking it for one viewer

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor #4A5568
  LifeLineBorderColor #7C86A0
  ParticipantBorderColor #7C86A0
  ParticipantBackgroundColor #FDFDFD
}

actor Moderator
participant "Backend\n(Spring Boot)" as API
participant "ffmpeg" as FF
database "PostgreSQL" as DB
participant "Browser\n(hls.js)" as Browser
actor Viewer

box "Server environment only" #F7F8FA
  participant API
end box

== 1. Encoding — happens once, per recording ==

Moderator -> API : upload a recording
activate API

API -> API : contentKey = 16 random bytes\n**AES-128**
note right
  A fresh key **per recording**.
  One leaked key then costs one
  meeting, not the whole archive.
end note

API -> API : wrapped = RSA-OAEP(**server public key**, contentKey)
API -> DB : store `videos.content_key` = wrapped
note right of DB
  Stored BEFORE encoding starts.
  A stored key with no segments is
  inert; segments with no stored key
  are unplayable forever.
end note

API -> FF : -hls_key_info_file <temp>
activate FF
note right of FF
  The plaintext key lives in a TEMP
  directory, never in the video folder —
  everything there is ingested into
  storage, so a key written there would
  be filed next to the segments it opens.
end note
FF -> FF : encrypt every segment (AES-128)
FF -> FF : write #EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI="key"
FF --> API : encrypted .ts segments + playlists
deactivate FF

API -> API : overwrite + delete the temp key
API -> DB : store segments (ciphertext)
deactivate API

note over DB
  **What a database dump now yields:**
  ciphertext segments + an RSA-wrapped key.
  The private key is NOT here — it is in the
  environment. That is the threat this addresses.
end note

== 2. Playback — happens per viewer, per session ==

Viewer -> Browser : press play
Browser -> API : GET .../index.m3u8?t=<ticket>
activate API
API -> API : rewrite every URI to carry the ticket\n— including the #EXT-X-KEY URI
note right
  The one URI in a playlist that is NOT
  on its own line. Missed, the key is
  fetched unauthenticated, refused, and
  playback fails with nothing on screen
  to explain it.
end note
API --> Browser : playlist
deactivate API

Browser -> Browser : generate an **RSA keypair** (WebCrypto)\nprivate half never leaves the tab

Browser -> API : GET .../key?t=<ticket>&**pk=<browser public key>**
activate API
API -> DB : read wrapped content key
API -> API : contentKey = RSA-decrypt(**server private key**, wrapped)
API -> API : sealed = RSA-OAEP(**browser public key**, contentKey)
API --> Browser : 200, body = sealed (256 bytes)\nCache-Control: no-store
deactivate API

note over API, Browser
  The raw AES key never crosses the network.
  Only a **public key** goes out and a **ciphertext**
  comes back — so a TLS-terminating proxy, CDN or
  WAF sees nothing usable.
end note

Browser -> Browser : contentKey = RSA-decrypt(**browser private key**, sealed)
Browser -> API : GET .../seg_00028.ts?t=<ticket>
API --> Browser : encrypted segment
Browser -> Browser : AES-128 decrypt, decode, render
Browser --> Viewer : video plays

== What this does NOT protect against ==

note over Viewer
  The browser must hold the plaintext AES key
  to decrypt the video, so **the viewer can read
  it** — devtools, or ffmpeg/yt-dlp, which handle
  AES-128 HLS automatically.

  This is the DRM problem. No key exchange solves
  it; only Widevine/FairPlay, where decryption
  happens inside a black box the user cannot open.
end note

@enduml
